From: vincek@intergalact.com (Vince Kerchner)
Date: Tue, 6 Dec 1994 17:47:56 -0800
Subject: [#94-12-269] SING: OK, now I get it

Thanks very much to Peter McCluskey and Gregory Sullivan for their recent
kind and informative posts about the singularity!  I think I have a much
better idea of what this event is all about, and how it differs from other
topics such as nanotech and uploading.

Peter C. McCluskey <pcm@rahul.net> sez:

> Yes, it is ill-defined, and Arthur Clarke may well have provided the
>original inspiration behind the concept.

I'm glad someone finally admits this, rather than continuing to use the
inherent confusion about this concept to promote their own, potentially
unrelated, "techno-rapture" ideas.   What's the Clarke reference you're
thinking of (the one that comes to mind is "The Nine Billion Names of God"
(or, I suppose, "2001"))?

> I don't expect anything that will look like an instantaneous change
>from the viewpoint of the first uploads (other than disorientation -:),
>or which will cause civilization to completely vanish to outside observers.

I agree, and suggest the name "gradularity" rather than "singularity".
Given all of the often overlooked problems inherent in near-infinite
computation, uploading, and nanotech, it seems to me that this transition
will take place over many years, if not many hundreds of years.  I don't
envision a day when transcendent technology will suddenly be available to
the masses, but instead a series of very small successes in these areas
over time.  I expect the first uploads will be very slow and crude, and
will be more mental "prosthetics" than wholesale personality transfer.  I
expect a similar series of limited advances for nanotech.  Alas, however, I
don't expect much of this by 2030.

sullivan@blaze.cs.jhu.edu (Gregory Sullivan) sez:

>If you are interested in what Vinge has to say about the singularity you can
>read:
>
>Magazine: Whole Earth
>Issue: December 10, 1993
>Title: Technological Singularity
>Author: Vernor Vinge

Thanks for the reference!  Based on the excerpt you posted, and on other
Vinge material, I now envision the singularity not as a time-based
transition, but as an abstraction in the evolution of intelligence, very
much like the punctuated equilibrium of biological natural selection.

For example, one might classify the cladistic levels of intelligence on
this planet along the following lines:

  1. Non-computational inanimate matter.
  2. Non-sentient biological matter.  This level involves computation (DNA
     replication, transcription, etc...) and (as Vinge points out in the
     Whole Earth excerpt) intelligence.  Not much intelligence, but a
     problem-solving capability on an evolutionary (i.e. millions of years)
     timescale.
  3. Non-sentient informational constructs.  Artificial intelligence:
     limited problem-solving capability, but neither aware nor self-aware.
  4. Sentient biological matter.  E.g. mammals.  Aware, and possibly
     self-aware.  Intelligent with limited problem-solving capability
     of an individual organism.
  5. Sapient biological matter.  E.g. us.  Presumably aware, self-aware,
     and facile with concepts such as "self-aware".
  6. Collective bio-informational aggregates.  E.g. society.  Composed of
     interacting sapient biological entities and non-sentient informational
     constructs.  What Ayn Rand might call "a collective, nameless,
     faceless, evil, residing in no one, but existing everywhere", and
     what Kevin Kelly might call "neo-biological civilization".  Unknown
     if either sentient or sapient.

To this list we might then add:

  7. Technologically augmented sapient biologicals, sentient and
     sapient informational constructs.

Given the coexistence of examples from list 1-6, and the evolutionary
timescales involved in going from one level to another, it seems reasonable
to me to assume that

  (a) All levels 1-7 can coexist simultaneously on this planet, and
  (b) It may take another evolutionary timescale to produce level 7.

Therefore, I see no reason to postulate the existence of a discontinuity or
"singularity", neither in magnitude nor in timescale, as a necessary
prerequisite or consequence of this development.

--Vince

