From: vincek@intergalact.com (Vince Kerchner)
Date: Wed, 7 Dec 1994 02:10:26 -0800
Subject: [#94-12-270] SING: Does it require uploads or nanotech?

Godshatter <mentat@telerama.lm.com> sez:

>Who said anything about uploads being required?  That seems to be just an
>assumption you drew out of thin air.

Everyone else seems to be talking about uploads being required.  What is
your version of the upload-less singularity?

>If you could do as much thinking in a
>microsecond as someone else could do in a second, you think you wouldn't notice
>things that escape the slower thinker?

How do you propose to increase congnition speed by 1e6 without uploads?

>Besides, I don't think it's [nanotech's] necessary for the singularity to
>start.

What is your version of the nanotech-less singularity?

In general, while I don't see a need for nanotech in order to have the kind
of augmented human society that most people seem to envision, I do see a
need for significant artificial mental enhancement.  While this may not
require full uploading, per se, I think it does require (a) a much more
intimate human-computer interface, and concomitant understanding of how
both the brain and the mind operate, than is currently available, and (b)
increases in raw computing power on the order of 1e6 to 1e9, if not more.
Whether the trillion megaflops required to approximate the computational
complexity of the brain are provided serially or in parallel, they still
need to be there in some form.

>I think that perhaps Perry and also Hans Moravec have placed too much emphasis
>on speed as a measure of intelligence; do we really think faster than chimps
>or just better?

Good point.  I would guess that a billion-fold augmented chimp would just
be concerned with eating that many more bananas, not with producing an
equivalent of the collected works of Shakespeare.  However, an interesting
experiment would be to suitably augment, or fully upload, a chimp (or a
collection of interacting chimps), and see if they evolve human-equivalent
intelligence.  A "speedup" of 1e9 would compress 10 million years of such
evolution into about 4 days.  And yet, I don't think that any such
evolution would happen.  It seems to me that there are many differences
between a simian brain and mind and those of a human, other than just speed
of thought.  Likewise, I do not expect that human augmentation in this
manner will lead to any new order of intelligence.

>Still, if you are thinking
>stupidly or just inefficiently, speeding up that process will probably just
>yield more stupid and inefficient thinking.

The same problem happens with computers today: faster processors do not
necessarily yield better results.  They may simply make the same mistakes
faster.

>Since intelligence is the
>basis of technological development, then it starts looking to me like a
>positive feedback system with intelligence feeding more efficient faster
>technological development whose effect is greater intelligence, etc.

While it may be true that greater intelligence provides better solutions to
technological problems, you have yet to demonstrate the converse.  In some
specific cases, this is certainly true: computers are excellent tools for
intellectual exploration, when used by people who are *already*
intelligent.  However, consider the great number of poeple who are
computer-illiterate, and for whom this technology is completely
ineffectual.  Furthermore, technology, and tools in general, serve to
eliminate selection pressures which are required for evolution.  In current
society, this has the negative effect that, while a small percentage of
people are getting smarter as a result of technology use, the majority of
the population is either unaffected, or may actually be getting dumber.  In
any case, it is not the direct application of technology which increases
intelligence, it is the indirect effect of having fewer physical burdens,
more free time, and the ability to explore areas of research which would
otherwise be inaccessible.

"Thomas H. Smith" <abner@bga.com> sez:

>Just one quick question...how much will the need for computing power be
>reduced by the fact that an uploaded person won't need to control
>his/her/it's breathing, digestion, cellular repair, etc?

This is a good argument against the blind copying of neural architecture
during the uploading process.  While presumably little of the cortex is
involved in purely autonomic bodily functions, much of it is mapped
directly to the afferent and efferent structure of the central and
peripheral nervous system.  Why carry all that along?  With more informed
understanding of both the structure of the cortex and its influence on the
operation of the mind, we should be able to isolate those neural subunits
(both physical and logical) which are important to us, and disregard the
rest.

nancy@genie.slhs.udel.edu (Nancy Lebovitz) sez:

>Very plausible, but I have a much more modest notion of the Singularity--
>I think that we'll have one as soon as there's a way of noticably
>(and reasonably cheaply) increasing people's intelligence.

I agree.  However, if such an increase is an incremental one (as I
anticipate), then I challenge the term "singularity".

>Maybe not with my definition (moderate increase of intelligence for
>the whole species). It still might only take a generation or so.

This depends on the nature of intelligence.  If there is a biological,
evolutionary, basis for intelligence (as there appears to be), then this
process will take many generations.  Technology will not do the
evolutionary work for us, it will only provide/remove the selection
pressures which influence reproduction and mutation.  Technology can act as
an amplifier of genetic diversity, but not as a source of that diversity
(in this context).

>The important difference would be in having a multimillenial time
>scale in individual lives.

If you mean an augmented lifespan, then yes.  Major problems with the
evolution of intelligence today include (a) just when we're beginning to
figure things out, we die, and (b) we have no way to pass on our
accumulated knowledge and experience to others (especially progeny) without
a long and tedious serial education process.  We have already passed the
point where our rate of overall combined knowledge acquisition exceeds our
ability to process and integrate it within our "natural" lifespan.

>Here's what life will *really* be like after the Singularity.

Thanks for the description ;-).

>Compatibility problems may well be what turns those exponential
>curves into s-curves.

I suspect there are other factors at work.  Both sigmoidal and
overshoot-and-collapse behavior are well-documented examples of
second-order dynamical systems.

--Vince

